= Solution
{parent=a}

Move the constant term to the left and factor the <cubic polynomial>:
$$
2x^3+9x^2-6x-5=(x-1)(2x+1)(x+5).
$$
Setting each factor to zero gives all three <polynomial roots>:
$$
\boxed{x=1,\quad-\frac12,\quad-5.}
$$

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <quadratic formula> gives the two <polynomial roots> $(-3\pm\sqrt5)/2$. Its leading coefficient is positive, so the <quadratic polynomial> is strictly negative between them. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\frac{-3-\sqrt5}{2}<x<\frac{-3+\sqrt5}{2}.}
$$

= 3
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

Matching the two <parametric equations> at an intersection gives $\cos\phi=-t$ and $\sin\phi=1+t$. The <Pythagorean trigonometric identity> therefore requires
$$
t^2+(1+t)^2=1,\qquad2t(t+1)=0.
$$
For $t=0$, the <sine> is one and the <cosine> is zero; for $t=-1$, the <cosine> is one and the <sine> is zero. Keeping the stated half-open angular interval gives
$$
\boxed{(t,\phi)=(0,\pi/2)\quad\text{or}\quad(-1,0).}
$$
The corresponding points are $(0,1)$ and $(2,0)$; both satisfy the original <parametric equations>.

