Use <integration by parts>, taking $u=x$ and $dv=\sin3x\,dx$:
$$
\int_0^{\pi/6}x\sin3x\,dx=\left[-\frac x3\cos3x+\frac19\sin3x\right]_0^{\pi/6}
=\boxed{\frac19.}
$$

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Since $d(\sin^2x)/dx=2\sin x\cos x=\sin2x$, <integration by substitution> $u=\sin^2x$ immediately gives
$$
\boxed{\int(\sin2x)e^{\sin^2x}\,dx=e^{\sin^2x}+C.}
$$
Here $C$ is an arbitrary <constant of integration>.

