The two labelled regions in the original diagram are complementary <circular sectors>. Their <areas> add to $\pi r^2$ and satisfy $A=2B$, so $B=\pi r^2/3$. The sector with central <angle> $\theta$ has <area> $r^2\theta/2$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\theta=\frac{2\pi}{3}=120^\circ.}
$$

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The two radii enclose the <angle> $2\pi/3$, so the triangle has <area> $T=\tfrac12r^2\sin(2\pi/3)=\sqrt3r^2/4$. From the sector calculation, $r^2=3B/\pi$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{T=\frac{3\sqrt3}{4\pi}\,B.}
$$

= 6
