= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A space diagonal of the unit <cube> has displacement <vector> $(1,1,1)$. Its Euclidean <norm>, or two successive applications of the <Pythagorean theorem>, gives
$$
\boxed{d=\sqrt{1^2+1^2+1^2}=\sqrt3\text{ units}.}
$$
The requested diagonal joins opposite vertices; $\sqrt2$ would be the diagonal of a face.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Choose two distinct space-diagonal directions $\mathbf u=(1,1,1)$ and $\mathbf v=(-1,1,1)$. Their <dot product> is one and their <norms> are both $\sqrt3$. Thus the acute <angle> between their unoriented lines is
$$
\boxed{\theta=\arccos\frac13\simeq70.53^\circ.}
$$
Any two distinct space diagonals have an absolute <dot product> of one when represented with components $\pm1$, so they give the same acute <angle>.

