Equating the two <polynomials> gives $x^3-3x^2+3x-1=(x-1)^3=0$. Thus the only intersection has $x=1$ and $y=4$:
$$
\boxed{(1,4).}
$$

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The two <derivatives> are $3x^2+3$ and $6x$, both equal to six at $x=1$. Their common <tangent line> through $(1,4)$ is $y-4=6(x-1)$, or $y=6x-2$. Setting $x=0$ gives its intersection with the vertical axis:
$$
\boxed{(0,-2).}
$$

= 11
{parent=Paper 1}
