= Solution
{parent=i}

The region is an unbounded wedge in the first quadrant, lying between the rays $y=x/2$ and $y=x$. Both boundaries and the origin are included. For integration in the opposite order, the same region is $y\ge0$ with $y\le x\le2y$.

\Image[paper-1-2018-q12-integration-wedge.png]
{title=The integration wedge and representative vertical and horizontal slices}

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For fixed $x>0$, integrate first over $y$:
$$
I=\int_0^\infty\left[\int_{x/2}^{x}xe^{-xy}\,dy\right]dx
=\int_0^\infty\left(e^{-x^2/2}-e^{-x^2}\right)dx.
$$
The integrand is nonnegative, so this order of the <double integral> is legitimate even before its finiteness is known. Applying the scaled <Gaussian integral> $\int_0^\infty e^{-ax^2}\,dx=\sqrt\pi/(2\sqrt a)$ yields
$$
\boxed{I=\frac{\sqrt\pi}{2}(\sqrt2-1).}
$$

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Reverse the order in the nonnegative <double integral>, using $0\le y<\infty$ and $y\le x\le2y$. For $y>0$, an <antiderivative> in $x$ is $-(x/y+1/y^2)e^{-xy}$. Thus
$$
I=\int_0^\infty\left[\left(1+\frac1{y^2}\right)e^{-y^2}
-\left(2+\frac1{y^2}\right)e^{-2y^2}\right]dy.
$$
Let $J$ denote the requested <improper integral>. Its numerator is $y^2+O(y^4)$ near zero, so its apparent singularity is removable; it also decays at infinity. We may therefore separate the preceding expression into the convergent integrals
$$
I=\int_0^\infty(e^{-y^2}-2e^{-2y^2})\,dy+J
=\frac{\sqrt\pi}{2}(1-\sqrt2)+J=-I+J.
$$
It follows that
$$
\boxed{J=\int_0^\infty\frac{e^{-x^2}-e^{-2x^2}}{x^2}\,dx=\sqrt\pi(\sqrt2-1).}
$$
The two terms divided separately by $x^2$ would each have a divergent <integral> at zero; the cancellation in their difference is essential.

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Scale the <ellipsoid> to a unit <ball> using
$$
x=ar\sin\theta\cos\phi,\qquad y=br\sin\theta\sin\phi,\qquad z=cr\cos\theta,
$$
with $0\le r\le1$, $0\le\theta\le\pi$ and $0\le\phi<2\pi$. This is <spherical coordinates> followed by a diagonal <linear transformation>, so the <Jacobian determinant> is $abc\,r^2\sin\theta$. The required <volume integral> is
$$
\boxed{V=abc\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^\pi\int_0^1r^2\sin\theta\,dr\,d\theta\,d\phi
=\frac{4\pi abc}{3}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <ellipsoid> and the exponential factor are invariant under separate reflections of any coordinate. Split the integrand into its two polynomial terms. The term $x^4y^2z$ times that exponential is odd in $z$, so its <volume integral> vanishes by reflecting $z\mapsto-z$. The other term $xy^2z^4$ times the exponential is odd in $x$, so its <volume integral> vanishes by reflecting $x\mapsto-x$. Both integrands are continuous on a bounded region. Therefore <odd-integrand cancellation by reflection> gives
$$
\boxed{\iiint_V(x^4y^2z+xy^2z^4)e^{-(x/a)^2-(y/b)^2-(z/c)^2}\,dx\,dy\,dz=0.}
$$

= 13
{parent=Paper 1}
