= a
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For $x\ne0$, divide by $x^2$ to obtain $y'+y/x=1/x^2$, a first-order <linear ordinary differential equation>. An <integrating factor> is $x$ on either interval not crossing zero, since the <product rule> gives
$$
(xy)'=\frac1x.
$$
Integration and division by $x$ give the explicit <general solution>
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac{\ln|x|+C}{x},\qquad x\ne0.}
$$
The original equation would read $0=1$ at zero, so no solution can include that point.

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Rewrite the <linear ordinary differential equation> as $y'+y=e^{3x}$. Multiplication by its <integrating factor> $e^x$ gives $(e^xy)'=e^{4x}$. Integrating yields
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac14e^{3x}+Ce^{-x}.}
$$
The <general solution> consists of this <particular solution> and the <homogeneous solution> $Ce^{-x}$.

= c
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The original PDF has $8y^3-4x$ in the denominator and $dy/dx$ on the left. Rearrange it into the <differential form>
$$
(5x+4y)\,dx+(4x-8y^3)\,dy=0.
$$
The cross <partial derivatives> of its coefficients are both four, so it is an <exact differential form>. Integrating its first coefficient in $x$ gives a potential $F=\tfrac52x^2+4xy+h(y)$. Matching $F_y=4x-8y^3$ gives $h(y)=-2y^4$. Therefore an implicit <general solution> is
$$
\boxed{\frac52x^2+4xy-2y^4=C.}
$$
Differentiating verifies $y'=(5x+4y)/(8y^3-4x)$. A solution graph is understood only on intervals where its denominator is nonzero; the printed quotient does not define a <derivative> when $8y^3=4x$.

= d
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For real solutions with $x>0$, the square root requires $y\ge0$. First retain the solution $y\equiv0$, which would be lost by dividing by a square root of $y$.

For $y>0$, use the <homogeneous first-order differential equation> substitution $v=\sqrt{y/x}>0$, so $y=xv^2$, $\sqrt{xy}=xv$ and $y'=v^2+2xvv'$. The equation becomes
$$
(1+v)(v^2+2xvv')=v^2,
\qquad\frac{2(1+v)}{v^2}\,dv=-\frac{dx}{x}.
$$
This is now a <separable differential equation>. Integration gives $-2/v+2\ln v=-\ln x+C$. Since $xv^2=y$, the positive solutions satisfy
$$
\boxed{\ln y-2\sqrt{\frac xy}=C\quad(y>0),\qquad\text{together with }y\equiv0.}
$$
The implicit family is sufficient here because only parts (a) and (b) request an explicit expression. It also shows why the nonnegative choice of $v$ matters: $\sqrt{xy}$ denotes the positive square root.

