= Solution
{parent=a}

The <exponential function> is strictly positive, so the zero <level set> is determined entirely by its prefactor:
$$
\boxed{xy=\frac14,\qquad\text{equivalently }y=\frac1{4x}\ (x\ne0).}
$$
This is a rectangular <hyperbola> with branches in the first and third quadrants.

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The sign of $f$ is the sign of $1-4xy$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{f>0\text{ when }xy<\frac14,\qquad f<0\text{ when }xy>\frac14.}
$$
The positive region includes both coordinate axes, the second and fourth quadrants, and the portions of the first and third quadrants on the origin side of the <hyperbola>. The two negative regions lie beyond its branches. The left-hand panel shows these regions; its markers locate the <stationary points> calculated next.

\Image[paper-1-2018-q14-signs-contours.png]
{title=Sign regions, stationary points and completed contour map of the Gaussian-weighted function}

= c
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Set both components of the <gradient> to zero. After dividing out the nonzero exponential, they are equivalent to
$$
x(1-4xy)+2y=0,\qquad y(1-4xy)+2x=0.
$$
Adding and subtracting gives $(x+y)(3-4xy)=0$ and $(x-y)(1+4xy)=0$. If $x=y$, the first equation reduces to $x(3-4x^2)=0$, giving the origin and the two diagonal points. If $x=-y$, it reduces to $x(4x^2-1)=0$, giving the origin and the two opposite-diagonal points. The alternative simultaneous values $xy=3/4$ and $xy=-1/4$ are incompatible, so these cases exhaust the possibilities. Thus the five <stationary points> are
$$
\boxed{(0,0),\quad\left(\frac{\sqrt3}{2},\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\right),\quad\left(-\frac{\sqrt3}{2},-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\right),\quad
\left(\frac12,-\frac12\right),\quad\left(-\frac12,\frac12\right).}
$$
They are marked in both panels of the figure.

= d
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Evaluate the <Hessian matrix> at the five <stationary points>. At the origin it is
$$
H(0,0)=\begin{pmatrix}-2&-4\\-4&-2\end{pmatrix},
$$
whose <eigenvalues> are $-6$ and $2$, so the origin is a <saddle point>. At the two points with $x=y=\pm\sqrt3/2$,
$$
H=e^{-3/2}\begin{pmatrix}10&2\\2&10\end{pmatrix},
$$
whose <eigenvalues> are $12e^{-3/2}$ and $8e^{-3/2}$. Both are positive, so these are strict <local minima>. At the two points with $x=-y=\pm1/2$,
$$
H=e^{-1/2}\begin{pmatrix}-6&-2\\-2&-6\end{pmatrix},
$$
whose <eigenvalues> are $-8e^{-1/2}$ and $-4e^{-1/2}$. Both are negative, so these are strict <local maxima>. In summary,
$$
\boxed{(0,0):\text{ saddle};\quad x=y=\pm\sqrt3/2:\text{ minima};\quad x=-y=\pm1/2:\text{ maxima}.}
$$
The quadratic changes along the <orthogonal> directions $(1,1)$ and $(1,-1)$ also provide a direct justification of these classifications.

= e
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The completed <contour plot> is shown in the right-hand panel. Its geometry follows from the symmetries $f(-x,-y)=f(x,y)$ and $f(y,x)=f(x,y)$, together with $f\to0$ as $x^2+y^2\to\infty$. For orientation, the two <local minima> have value $-2e^{-3/2}$, the two <local maxima> have value $2e^{-1/2}$, and the <saddle point> has value one.

Negative <level sets> between $-2e^{-3/2}$ and zero form separate closed curves around the two <local minima> in the first and third quadrants. Positive <level sets> below one form a single closed curve enclosing the origin and both <local maxima>. At level one, a critical <level set> crosses itself at the origin and surrounds the two <local maxima> in two lobes. Between one and $2e^{-1/2}$, the <level sets> split into separate closed curves around those <local maxima>. The zero <level set> remains the two unbounded branches $xy=1/4$.

Near the origin, the <Taylor expansion> is $f=1-x^2-4xy-y^2+O((x^2+y^2)^2)$. Consequently the crossing directions at the saddle height satisfy $y=(-2\pm\sqrt3)x$, giving the local shape of the critical <level set>.

= 15
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The first $n$ terms of <Taylor's theorem>, with the <Taylor remainder> in mean-value form, are
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!}(x-a)^k+R_n(x),\qquad R_n(x)=\frac{f^{(n)}(\xi)}{n!}(x-a)^n,}
$$
where $\xi$ lies between $a$ and $x$ for $x\ne a$. Sufficient hypotheses are continuous <derivatives> through order $n-1$ on the interval between the endpoints and an $n$th <derivative> in its interior. At $x=a$ the remainder is zero. The finite <Taylor polynomial> ends at degree $n-1$; this identity does not require convergence of an infinite <Taylor series>.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Since $a_0=f(0)\ne0$, write $1/f(x)=b_0+b_1x+b_2x^2+O(x^3)$ and multiply the two <power series>. Equating coefficients with the identity $f(x)/f(x)=1$ gives
$$
a_0b_0=1,\qquad a_0b_1+a_1b_0=0,\qquad a_0b_2+a_1b_1+a_2b_0=0.
$$
Solving successively yields
$$
\boxed{\frac1{f(x)}=\frac1{a_0}-\frac{a_1}{a_0^2}x+\frac{a_1^2-a_0a_2}{a_0^3}x^2+O(x^3).}
$$
These are the constant, linear and quadratic terms; particular coefficients may vanish. For a convergent <power series> defining $f$ near zero, continuity and $a_0\ne0$ ensure its reciprocal is also defined in a neighborhood of zero.

= c
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Multiply the <Maclaurin series> of <hyperbolic cosine> by the <binomial series> for the reciprocal square root:
$$
\cosh x=1+\frac{x^2}{2}+\frac{x^4}{24}+O(x^6),\qquad
(1+x^2)^{-1/2}=1-\frac{x^2}{2}+\frac{3x^4}{8}+O(x^6).
$$
The quadratic terms cancel, while the quartic coefficient is $1/24-1/4+3/8=1/6$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\frac{\cosh x}{\sqrt{1+x^2}}=1+\frac{x^4}{6}+O(x^6).}
$$
The absence of odd powers follows from the function's even symmetry.

= d
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Combine the <logarithms> first and factor out the limiting constant:
$$
\ln(1+x+2x^2)-\ln x^2=\ln\left(2+\frac1x+\frac1{x^2}\right)
=\ln2+\ln\left(1+\frac1{2x}+\frac1{2x^2}\right).
$$
Apply the <Taylor expansion> $\ln(1+u)=u-u^2/2+O(u^3)$ with $u=1/(2x)+1/(2x^2)$. The first three nonzero terms, including the constant, are
$$
\boxed{\ln(1+x+2x^2)-\ln x^2=\ln2+\frac1{2x}+\frac3{8x^2}+O(x^{-3}),\qquad x\to\infty.}
$$
This is an <asymptotic expansion> in reciprocal powers of $x$.

= 16
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <slope> of the particle's straight trajectory is $\tan\theta_i=y_i/x_0$, so
$$
\boxed{y_i=x_0\tan\theta_i.}
$$
The endpoint directions $\pm\pi/2$ are parallel to the screen and do not give finite collisions, but each has zero <probability> in the continuous angular model. The relation therefore holds <almost surely>.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The angular interval has width $\pi$, so its <continuous uniform distribution> has <probability density function>
$$
\boxed{g(\theta)=\begin{cases}1/\pi,&-\pi/2<\theta<\pi/2,\\0,&\text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}}
$$
Integrating this <probability density function> gives the <cumulative distribution function>
$$
\boxed{G(\theta)=\begin{cases}0,&\theta\le-\pi/2,\\\frac12+\theta/\pi,&-\pi/2<\theta<\pi/2,\\1,&\theta\ge\pi/2.\end{cases}}
$$
Values of the <probability density function> at the two individual endpoints may be assigned arbitrarily without changing any <probability>.

= c
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <tangent function> is strictly increasing on the angular interval. Thus $Y\le y$ is equivalent, up to the zero-probability endpoints, to $\Theta\le\arctan(y/x_0)$. Substituting into the angular <cumulative distribution function> gives
$$
\boxed{F(y)=\frac12+\frac1\pi\arctan\frac y{x_0},\qquad-\infty<y<\infty.}
$$
Its <limits> are zero and one at the two infinite endpoints. This is the construction in which the <tangent of a uniform angle has a Cauchy distribution>.

= d
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Differentiate the <cumulative distribution function> using the <chain rule>:
$$
\boxed{f(y)=F'(y)=\frac1{\pi x_0}\frac1{1+(y/x_0)^2}=\frac{x_0}{\pi(x_0^2+y^2)}.}
$$
This is a <Cauchy distribution> with location zero and scale $x_0$. The <probability density function> is nonnegative and integrates to $F(\infty)-F(-\infty)=1$.

= e
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The <expected value> is not defined: the integral of $yf(y)$ over the positive half-line diverges logarithmically to $+\infty$, and the negative half-line gives $-\infty$. In particular, symmetry alone does not supply a mean. More explicitly,
$$
\int_0^R yf(y)\,dy=\frac{x_0}{2\pi}\ln\left(1+\frac{R^2}{x_0^2}\right)\longrightarrow\infty.
$$
The second <moment> also diverges because $y^2f(y)\to x_0/\pi>0$ as $|y|\to\infty$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\text{the mean, variance and standard deviation are undefined}.}
$$
The symmetric <Cauchy principal value> of the first-moment integral is zero, but it is not an <expected value> and does not define a <variance>.

= f
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

The new angular interval has width $\pi/2$, so its <probability density function> is $2/\pi$ there. Its endpoints stay away from the <poles> of the <tangent function>, making $Y$ bounded and its <expected value> finite. Integrate over the <continuous uniform distribution> of angles:
$$
E[Y]=\frac{2x_0}{\pi}\int_{-\pi/6}^{\pi/3}\tan\theta\,d\theta
=\frac{2x_0}{\pi}[-\ln(\cos\theta)]_{-\pi/6}^{\pi/3}.
$$
The two <cosines> are $\sqrt3/2$ and $1/2$. Hence
$$
\boxed{E[Y]=\frac{2x_0}{\pi}\ln\sqrt3=\frac{x_0\ln3}{\pi}.}
$$
It has units of <length> and is positive because the permitted positive angles extend farther than the negative ones.

= g
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=g}

The $i$th screen distance is $x_0/2^i$, starting at $i=1$. The previous <expected value> therefore scales to $E[Y_i]=x_0\ln3/(\pi2^i)$. To justify the infinite <linearity of expectation>, note that $|\tan\theta_i|\le\sqrt3$ throughout the restricted angular interval. Thus
$$
\sum_{i=1}^\infty|Y_i|\le\sqrt3x_0\sum_{i=1}^\infty2^{-i}=\sqrt3x_0.
$$
This deterministic integrable bound justifies the <expectation of an absolutely summable random series>, even without assuming independent firing angles. Summing the <geometric series> gives
$$
\boxed{E\left[\sum_{i=1}^\infty Y_i\right]=\frac{x_0\ln3}{\pi}\sum_{i=1}^\infty2^{-i}=\frac{x_0\ln3}{\pi}.}
$$

= 17
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use <integration by substitution> $t=\tan x$, so $dt=dx/\cos^2x$. The <indefinite integral> becomes $\int dt/[t(t^2-1)]$. Its <partial fraction decomposition> is
$$
\frac1{t(t^2-1)}=-\frac1t+\frac12\frac1{t-1}+\frac12\frac1{t+1}.
$$
Integrating gives $-\ln|t|+\tfrac12\ln|t^2-1|+C$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\int\frac{dx}{\cos^2x(\tan^3x-\tan x)}=\frac12\ln\left|1-\frac1{\tan^2x}\right|+C.}
$$
The formula applies on each interval where the printed integrand is defined: $\cos x\ne0$ and $\tan x\notin\{0,1,-1\}$. The <constant of integration> may differ between such intervals.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $n>1$, <integration by parts> with $u=x^{n-1}$ and $dv=e^{-x}\,dx$ yields
$$
I_n=[-x^{n-1}e^{-x}]_0^\infty+(n-1)\int_0^\infty x^{n-2}e^{-x}\,dx=(n-1)I_{n-1}.
$$
The boundary term vanishes at zero because $n-1>0$, and at infinity because exponential decay dominates every fixed power. The initial <improper integral> is $I_1=\int_0^\infty e^{-x}\,dx=1$. Iterating the <recurrence relation> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{I_n=(n-1)(n-2)\cdots1\,I_1=(n-1)!,\qquad n\ge1.}
$$
This is the positive-integer case of the <Gamma function> identity $\Gamma(n)=(n-1)!$.

= c
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Differentiate $x/(1+x^2)^n$ and integrate between zero and infinity. Its boundary values vanish for every integer $n\ge1$, giving
$$
0=\int_0^\infty\left[\frac1{(1+x^2)^n}-\frac{2nx^2}{(1+x^2)^{n+1}}\right]dx.
$$
Since $x^2/(1+x^2)^{n+1}=(1+x^2)^{-n}-(1+x^2)^{-n-1}$, this becomes $0=E_n-2n(E_n-E_{n+1})$. Hence the <recurrence relation> is
$$
\boxed{E_{n+1}=\frac{2n-1}{2n}E_n.}
$$
The starting value is $E_1=[\arctan x]_0^\infty=\pi/2$. Therefore $E_2=\pi/4$, $E_3=3\pi/16$, and
$$
\boxed{\int_0^\infty\frac{dx}{(1+x^2)^4}=E_4=\frac56\frac34\frac12\frac\pi2=\frac{5\pi}{32}.}
$$
These are also <Wallis integrals> after the <integration by substitution> $x=\tan t$.

