= a
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The middle row is $-1/2$ times the sum of the first and third rows, so the rows are linearly dependent and the <determinant> is zero. The <matrix trace> is the sum of diagonal entries, $1+4+1=6$. A <transpose> preserves the <determinant>, while the <determinant> of a product is the product of its <determinants>. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\det A=0,\quad\operatorname{Tr}A=6,\quad\det(A^{\mathsf T})=0,\quad\det(A^2)=0.}
$$

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <matrix> is symmetric under interchange of the first and third coordinates. For an antisymmetric <vector> $(1,0,-1)$, direct multiplication gives $A(1,0,-1)^{\mathsf T}=-6(1,0,-1)^{\mathsf T}$. For a symmetric <vector> $(p,q,p)$, the <linear transformation> reduces to
$$
\begin{pmatrix}p\\q\end{pmatrix}\longmapsto\begin{pmatrix}8&-4\\-8&4\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}p\\q\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The reduced <characteristic polynomial> is $\lambda(\lambda-12)$. At $\lambda=0$, its equations give $q=2p$; at $\lambda=12$, they give $q=-p$. Normalizing the three <eigenvectors> gives
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\lambda=-6:&\quad\mathbf v_-=(1,0,-1)^{\mathsf T}/\sqrt2,\\
\lambda=0:&\quad\mathbf v_0=(1,2,1)^{\mathsf T}/\sqrt6,\\
\lambda=12:&\quad\mathbf v_+=(1,-1,1)^{\mathsf T}/\sqrt3.
\end{aligned}}
$$
Their <norms> are one. Their unnormalized pairwise <dot products> are $1-1=0$, $1-1=0$ and $1-2+1=0$, proving mutual <orthogonality>. The <eigenvalues> sum to six and have product zero, agreeing with the <matrix trace> and <determinant> from part (a).

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The three <eigenvectors> form an <orthonormal basis>, so an arbitrary <vector> has expansion $\mathbf r=a\mathbf v_-+b\mathbf v_0+c\mathbf v_+$. Multiplication by $A$ removes the zero-eigenvalue component:
$$
A\mathbf r=-6a\mathbf v_-+12c\mathbf v_+.
$$
Its <dot product> with $\mathbf v_0$ is zero for every $a,b,c$. Thus one convenient answer is the nonzero <vector>
$$
\boxed{\mathbf u=(1,2,1)^{\mathsf T}.}
$$
Equivalently, $A^{\mathsf T}\mathbf u=A\mathbf u=0$, so $(A\mathbf r)\cdot\mathbf u=\mathbf r\cdot(A^{\mathsf T}\mathbf u)=0$. Any nonzero scalar multiple of this <vector> in the <null space> also works.

