= a
{parent=19}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For statement (1), <continuity> at the interior point means
$$
\boxed{\lim_{x\to x_0}f(x)=f(x_0).}
$$
Explicitly, for every $\varepsilon>0$ there exists $\delta>0$ such that $|x-x_0|<\delta$ implies $|f(x)-f(x_0)|<\varepsilon$ for points in the domain. This is the <epsilon-delta definition of continuity>.

For statement (2), <differentiability> means the two-sided difference quotient has a finite <limit>:
$$
\boxed{f'(x_0)=\lim_{h\to0}\frac{f(x_0+h)-f(x_0)}h\in\mathbb R.}
$$
The same finite value must be obtained from positive and negative $h$. <Differentiability> implies <continuity>, whereas the converse need not hold.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $m=0$, <continuity> fails. Take $x_k=(\pi/2+2\pi k)^{-1}$ and $\widetilde x_k=(3\pi/2+2\pi k)^{-1}$. Both tend to zero, but the corresponding function values are $1$ and $-1$. Thus there is no <limit>, and the function is not <differentiable> either, since <differentiability> would imply <continuity>.

For $m=1$, the bound $|x\sin(1/x)|\le|x|$ and the <squeeze theorem> give <continuity> at zero. Its difference quotient is $\sin(1/h)$, however, which has no <limit> by the same two sequences. It is therefore not <differentiable> there.

For $m=2$, the bound $|x^2\sin(1/x)|\le x^2$ proves <continuity>. Its difference quotient is $h\sin(1/h)$, whose absolute value is bounded by $|h|$, so the <derivative> exists and equals zero. The complete classification is
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c|c}m&\text{continuous at }0&\text{differentiable at }0\\\hline0&\text{no}&\text{no}\\1&\text{yes}&\text{no}\\2&\text{yes}&\text{yes, }f_2'(0)=0\end{array}.}
$$

= b
{parent=19}
